    "https://external.jobs.uwa.edu.au/jobs/board-chair-australian-ska-regional-centre-aussrc-board-crawley-wa-australia": {
      "title": "Board Chair",
      "description": "Board Chair - Australian SKA Regional Centre (AusSRC) Board - Crawley, WA, Australia Back to UWA My Profile Kiosk mode Pause the proceeding carousel Board Chair - Australian SKA Regional Centre (AusSRC) Board 524246 Crawley, WA Casual Closing on: 6 Sep 2026 Add to favourites View favourites A unique opportunity to contribute to the governance of a nationally significant research infrastructure initiative supporting one of the most ambitious scientific projects ever undertaken. AusSRC is seeking expressions of interest from suitably qualified individuals to join as Chair of its Board. Remuneration: $35,000 per annum, plus allowance for domestic travel for Board meetings. About AusSRC The SKA Observatory (SKAO) is an intergovernmental organisation tasked with building and operating the world’s largest radio telescopes. In order to fully exploit the scientific output of the immense amount of data flowing from the telescopes, the SKAO is working with science communities across the world through SKA Regional Centres (SRCs), which are nationally led hubs that form the basis of a global compute and science support infrastructure. AusSRC is Australia’s portion of this international network and has been funded – by the Australian Government through the Department of Industry, Science, and Resources (DISR) – at a level of $63 million from FY 2022/23 to 2030/31. Its remit is to establish and build capability to support the Australian and international SKA science communities, thereby ushering in the next era of astronomical discovery and advanced data processing. AusSRC is a joint venture between CSIRO – Australia’s national science agency, Curtin University, the Pawsey Supercomputing Research Centre, and The University of Western Australia (UWA). AusSRC’s joint venture organisations formally collaborate through an unincorporated joint venture agreement (JVA), to support and develop AusSRC into a long-term national facility. Appointment The AusSRC JVA provides for a seven-member Board that is responsible for the operation and management of AusSRC and AusSRC funding. The Board comprises the Board Chair (independent; this opportunity), one representative from each of the JVA partners, and two independent Board members. The Chair appointment is Independent and therefore not employed by or acting as an agent for any of the Partners. In addition to the voting Board members, the Board includes two Board Advisors, representing SKAO and Astronomy Australia Ltd (AAL). These Advisors are invited at the discretion of the Chair to attend open Board meetings and advise in the context of domestic and international activities. Partner representative Board Members, Independent Board members, and Chairpersons are appointed for a term of up to three years and can be reappointed for a further term of three years pursuant to the JVA clauses. Independent Members are appointed for their specific expertise relevant to the functions of the Board. Board Chair appointments are through unanimous approval of the AusSRC Partner representatives. Responsibilities The Board Chair will play a key role in guiding AusSRC through its next phase of development as it builds national capability for SKA science support and advanced data processing. The role offers the opportunity to contribute to an internationally significant scientific endeavour while working with Australia’s leading research and infrastructure partners. The role of the Chair will be to: manage proceedings of the Board; authorise the issue of meeting notices to the Board; issue reports prepared for and on behalf of the Board to the Partners; provide support and guidance to the Director and, in consultation with the relevant employing line manager, review the performance of the Director; contribute to Board meeting discussions and advise in an expert capacity on AusSRC functions, budget, project plans, and resource allocation; support the Director in identifying opportunities for working with government, industry, science and technology groups to promote AusSRC’s objectives; contribute to approvals processes including reporting, operating protocols, terms of significant contracts; carry out other functions to assist with achievement of AusSRC’s objectives. Required Skills The Board Chair is required to have demonstrated skills and experience covering three or more of the following areas: Board or senior governance experience, ideally including Chair or committee leadership * Government relations and public policy * Finance and Audit * Legal and Risk Management * Astronomy and Scientific Research Large scale computing, Data and Research Infrastructure Service Delivery and End-user Engagement Industry Engagement * The skills with a * will be prioritised for this appointment. Time Commitment and Travel The AusSRC Board meets quarterly, twice in person (domestic locations) and twice online. Meetings typically run for 3 hours. In person meetings are supplemented with additional information sessions. Pre-reading and preparation for meetings is expected to take approximately 3-6 hours. In addition to the Board meetings, the Chair meets with the AusSRC Director between meetings as required. On occasion, out of session meetings may be required to resolve pressing issues. The successful appointee will be expected to travel to attend in-person meetings. How to apply Closing date: Sunday, 6 September 2026. Please apply online via the Apply Now button, attaching your resume and two professional references. The content of your resume and cover letter should demonstrate how you meet the required skills of the role. For further information about this role, please contact Professor Simon Ellingsen at Simon.Ellingsen@uwa.edu.au For more information on AusSRC please go to https://aussrc.org/ Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 1f6266fd Last Name (required) a1df220b Email (required) 0145f282 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Other jobs Solution Architect Crawley, WA Information Technology Partner with technical and business stakeholders to design, modernise and evolve the platforms that underpin UWA's operations. Senior Cybersecurity Architecture Specialist Crawley, WA Information Technology Join a high-performing team shaping UWA's cyber security future through secure-by-design and DevSecOps excellence. School Operations Officer (Music) Crawley, WA Student Administration / Support Services The UWA Conservatorium of Music is a dynamic and diverse hub for teaching, performance, research and community engagement. Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 110831e5 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Executive Executive Employment Type Casual Casual Locations Crawley, WA, Australia Crawley, WA, Australia First Name (required) 9dac9a5e Last Name (required) 66ff4fe7 Email (required) 17b32c4a Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Benefits Flexible working options considered. 26 weeks paid parental leave after one year and 36 weeks after five years continuous service, regardless of gender. 4 weeks annual leave with the option to purchase more. 13 weeks long service leave after seven/ten years. Salary packaging options. 17% superannuation, with the option to reduce to the minimum super guarantee. Up to 25% discount on eligible UWA full-fee courses. Discounted health insurance, and convenient on-campus childcare options. Incremental progression based on 12 months continuous service. Excluding casual positions. The University of Western Australia acknowledges that its campus is situated on Noongar land, and that Noongar people remain the spiritual and cultural custodians of their land, and continue to practise their values, languages, beliefs and knowledge. Indigenous commitment Facebook